      "problem": "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Find $2u+v.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we multiply each component of $u$ by $2.$ Then, we add the vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2u+v & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+3 \\\\ 6+1 \\\\ 4+4 \\\\ 8+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
